7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88582" :question/id #uuid "44ef4d16-5b7c-5c1c-ae5e-3f46913cf425" :question/math-academy-id "q-88582" :question/problem "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}-3$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =((-x)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({x}^{2}-3)$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-88582/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-88582"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259/selection/400216202e0be148587d73e779db8a40182419046bd350f6c62ae114fae98245" :answer/id #uuid "7693e9fc-715d-5d4d-8002-ce63e512ffd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "f2baff23-fbf6-5a97-ad1e-e555e37302b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259/selection/c3692cb0b86a35ecde176a0bdca7cf6ab800631cddf87c0acd91adcd5c3cd959" :answer/id #uuid "8c583a2e-1c81-531d-8430-3b23943e4231" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259/selection/6e4d2c3a1404932cb40ca57333ec660867539f9f4aa7e86a3e0a1ace25113cf8" :answer/id #uuid "8e068557-ac8e-588f-a9b7-df6e0c9cc732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259/selection/65511939096b484cbaa12e25b35e6cf35c66cf8b1426a764bb2219250b9e2db5" :answer/id #uuid "8bc11f47-51f1-5a8c-851f-3d243ecac4c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3aeaeec-0dd5-5acd-a556-85aa12c46f76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115259/selection/400216202e0be148587d73e779db8a40182419046bd350f6c62ae114fae98245" :answer-field/choices ["q-115259/selection/400216202e0be148587d73e779db8a40182419046bd350f6c62ae114fae98245" "q-115259/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-115259/selection/c3692cb0b86a35ecde176a0bdca7cf6ab800631cddf87c0acd91adcd5c3cd959" "q-115259/selection/6e4d2c3a1404932cb40ca57333ec660867539f9f4aa7e86a3e0a1ace25113cf8" "q-115259/selection/65511939096b484cbaa12e25b35e6cf35c66cf8b1426a764bb2219250b9e2db5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115259" :question/id #uuid "01023191-e11f-5c48-bf20-f38a6d28c5ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-115259" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((-x)^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115259/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115259"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "e05cf006-4e35-5836-9c93-3b8d7516c161" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "3ec32f9c-c87d-5acd-b4c3-87cd8d707d3b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "4da94543-36fc-50ab-9e04-f6a5248a7c65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "867a39fb-d4e9-5177-b01d-2d8e24a09ebb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "bf2aa493-2f19-5559-b2b5-b6fc6d11ec3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f459767b-797d-5350-848a-cf0ecc8e9d00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-88698/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-88698/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-88698/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-88698/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-88698/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-88698/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88698" :question/id #uuid "8c4ec3cb-2a21-5f14-9f3d-91d3ab95f323" :question/math-academy-id "q-88698" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-2 \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}+2) \\\\  & \\ne -h(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{5}+(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{5}-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{5}+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{(-x)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-88698/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-88698"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3b36ea2c-51c5-55cb-9634-5f1fc7cf3ad1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "89c5a5f4-5603-5cf5-ba87-8d510cdf568f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "8f05c1cc-9601-58e1-b22b-0c9137e0eba0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "71b9adf1-11c1-506e-a9c7-fc16dc13129e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "efce043f-c81a-5694-8512-ac98dc0580d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8acd680-1834-52d3-a1e2-a9009635a9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-88689/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-88689/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-88689/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-88689/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-88689/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-88689/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88689" :question/id #uuid "d5b8f26e-0fd6-5a4b-9564-e6d55b96cd5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88689" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-88689/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-88689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-88209"] :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{6}5{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=5{x}^{3}$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2{x}^{5}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2(-x)^{5} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{5} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}2{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "6b059072-2929-511d-ae52-4872fa0d647c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9d256e7f-9c96-5c13-beb9-4be28f2c02c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "504ba205-c349-541c-b744-4c22e4694fcb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "cc917853-2cff-50ea-b159-bcf4beeaf0dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "00023582-f48e-51ee-94c5-189c2664b183" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "55e12088-410d-56ff-8b61-0d283d8401f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-88208/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-88208/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-88208/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-88208/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-88208/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-88208/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-88208" :question/id #uuid "f8db7c7d-2c36-54ae-a136-cfed51754274" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-88208/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-88208"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "9c337d29-a21a-5b03-b99e-61c285bd81c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "d2f0bd08-7e18-5b27-a0db-6ece8beb467e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "f0fe3ddf-aa32-55cd-b995-26d5b599206e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "4121ca88-9649-5e27-92c5-fe01186c91d0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "fac589ec-0320-5beb-b3f0-d86ff9930e4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ba0e6c-800d-5c0d-bd49-ee01ed2b4ab4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115276/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-115276/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-115276/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-115276/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-115276/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-115276/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115276" :question/id #uuid "18225fca-59e9-551a-ae68-be859317b188" :question/math-academy-id "q-115276" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2}{3} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115276/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115276"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334354/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a59051da-7fc3-52c5-900e-90e4096b3bb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334354/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5003cd86-c816-5b76-8f3e-ad794b0ec3b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334354/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334354/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334354" :question/id #uuid "d1d28b87-b5c4-5756-ad04-9539f3fce14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-334354" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{3}}{1+(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334354/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334354"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334370/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "feb7c850-20be-5332-a5de-9d8c7897b38f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334370/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "90f2d0d8-960b-595b-a4c2-8e564e576ff0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334370/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb2ac4f7-d05e-5e07-be02-0051da36ac0d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334370/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334370/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-334370/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334370" :question/id #uuid "fb95c88c-49bb-5efb-9ee9-31b82db8dea3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334370" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334370/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334370"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334369/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "5cb4c30d-be67-5a09-befb-b8ae45f6dec9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334369/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3a2194c-858e-529f-b442-7281e9d6d9ec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334369/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334369/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334369" :question/id #uuid "db66b9a5-980d-5cdb-805e-4fe14da0f316" :question/math-academy-id "q-334369" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}-{e}^{x}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334369/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334352/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "63fd1c51-8dcb-5983-bd55-8c96cca2c5c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334352/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "4715a737-1300-553f-b813-e1b91d681149" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334352/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f892b3e0-f4dd-5fea-aa6d-f9030612390d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334352/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-334352/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-334352/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334352" :question/id #uuid "4c3b3633-3cca-52ce-9f8b-8508e90c46c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334352" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}sin\\,({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{1}{(-x)^{2}}sin\\,((-x)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334352/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334352"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188/selection/7f0f2478ce521aa4000523fe155bfe972c7a8cc195da49dd5eb0249ef8194675" :answer/id #uuid "4f23b530-decd-5d97-b0fa-de91d18d7097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188/selection/2975a59a267852917cc6f11a023dfb0a3a74e161efa53fb66f82b5f65ecee9d0" :answer/id #uuid "6ac3bdec-788f-5a7e-ac8a-125050356bed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188/selection/a5839b1e7c6d07232ae6f92a26fc3d7c628a71925b32d11fb1f159b763e5536f" :answer/id #uuid "b6e33888-b713-5a02-b5e5-6367d58fdd34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188/selection/8eb9e3bf1860d90d4d5da1878dcdab2c2ce316bc13d6c4c5c0bbaacaff112984" :answer/id #uuid "0ba48582-b318-544d-a31e-40eb3edd4a43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188/selection/82b9c7842f1c80636a82c5f7de2f8a6208cf7f8e3e3a4b6a98cd7fe14d5f8bb3" :answer/id #uuid "b6371d58-0924-581a-8c1a-a7affe56b7bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{A}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c2d0fba-0bbc-5b52-9fdd-9bd106fd1b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-156188/selection/8eb9e3bf1860d90d4d5da1878dcdab2c2ce316bc13d6c4c5c0bbaacaff112984" :answer-field/choices ["q-156188/selection/7f0f2478ce521aa4000523fe155bfe972c7a8cc195da49dd5eb0249ef8194675" "q-156188/selection/2975a59a267852917cc6f11a023dfb0a3a74e161efa53fb66f82b5f65ecee9d0" "q-156188/selection/a5839b1e7c6d07232ae6f92a26fc3d7c628a71925b32d11fb1f159b763e5536f" "q-156188/selection/8eb9e3bf1860d90d4d5da1878dcdab2c2ce316bc13d6c4c5c0bbaacaff112984" "q-156188/selection/82b9c7842f1c80636a82c5f7de2f8a6208cf7f8e3e3a4b6a98cd7fe14d5f8bb3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-156188" :question/id #uuid "a0b99c07-f690-5bcb-a878-0a11ff2111e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-156188" :question/problem "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=A,$ what is the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-x)}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{{e}^{-x}+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{0}\\frac{x}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-156188/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-156188"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10897"] :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx .\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) = x \\sin{x}$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2  x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10898"] :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =4(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & ={e}^{(-x)}+{e}^{-(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-10911"] :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A,$ what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x) =  x\\cos x$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x  \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+A & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{0}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
